@description REAPER MCP — Model Context Protocol interface (immersive/spatial audio)
@version 1.12.0
@author James Livingston
@provides
  [darwin] reaper_mcp.dylib https://github.com/inseglet-audio/inseglet/releases/download/v$version/reaper_mcp.dylib
@changelog
  Instrument release: Inseglet can now tell you whether a take's media is actually there.

  reaper://track/{trackIndex}/item/{itemIndex}/take/{takeIndex}/source is a new resource --
  the fourth -- carrying a per-source readout for one take: the ReaScript-API rows (file name,
  type, channel count, sample rate, length) alongside the SDK vtable rows, including
  PCM_source::IsAvailable(). IsAvailable is not in the ReaScript API at any version, so this is
  the only route to a take's source-validity state: online, offline, or missing. Read
  vtable.controlPassed before any vtable row -- when it is false, the SDK header and the running
  REAPER disagree about the vtable layout and no vtable figure is believable. The panel carries
  that control itself rather than letting the numbers quietly lie.

  The three accessor tools -- analysis.accessor_meter, analysis.accessor_levels and
  analysis.accessor_gaps -- now report source validity instead of papering over it. Each read
  gains a `sourceValidity` block: how many sources it interrogated, how many reported unavailable, the
  RAW sample rate / channel count / length the source gave, and `defaulted`, which NAMES every
  field a fallback stood in for. On a read that did not interrogate exactly one
  source -- every track read -- those three are NULL rather than 0, because a zero there would be
  the very substitution this release removes. When a source is unavailable the block also carries the remedy.

  *** This is ADDITIVE. No tool changes what it returns, refuses anything it used to accept, or
  reads different samples. ***

  The fix behind it is ours. The accessor substituted a plausible sample rate for a source that
  reported zero, and a channel count of 2 for one that reported less than one -- exactly the two
  fields REAPER zeroes when it cannot open a source. So the read looked identical whether the
  media was there or not, and three separate investigations concluded the datum did not exist.
  It did. A defensive default is a detector-killer: code that swaps a plausible value in for a
  missing one destroys the evidence that it was missing.

  The predicate now lives in its own REAPER-free header so its whole truth table is testable, and
  unit.source_probe carries two controls that must FIRE: the pre-fix defaults reproducing the
  identical-read null, and the predicate stripped of its quarter-note guard reporting an ordinary
  MIDI take as a broken source. The second is the expensive direction -- a detector that fires on
  healthy content is a detector its users switch off.

  Surface 190 tools / 4 resources / 5 prompts (was 190 / 3 / 5). ctest 27 -> 28.
  docs/REFERENCE.md is regenerated: it had not been regenerated when the resource landed.
